{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Combining algorithms{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <h1>One request, five algorithms</h1>
    <p>
        Every cell below is the same <code>AccessRequest</code> — <code>PUBLISH</code> on the same
        document, by you — put to the same voters. The only thing that moves is the third argument
        of <code>AccessControlManager::decide()</code>: the combining algorithm. A strategy is an
        argument of the question, not a setting of the application.
    </p>
    <dl>
        {% for name, description in strategies %}
            <dt><code>{{ name }}</code></dt>
            <dd class="muted">{{ description }}</dd>
        {% endfor %}
    </dl>
    <p class="muted">
        <code>two_keys</code> is this application's, <code>{{ custom }}</code>. It is a strategy for
        no other reason than implementing <code>StrategyInterface</code>:
        <code>access_control.strategy</code> is an autoconfigured tag, so it is reachable by name
        wherever the four shipped ones are.
    </p>

    {% for row in rows %}
        <h2>#{{ row.document.id }} · {{ row.document.title }}</h2>
        <p class="muted">
            Owned by <code>{{ row.document.owner }}</code>{% if row.document.locked %},
            <strong>locked by compliance</strong>{% endif %}.
        </p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Voter</th><th>Said</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                {% for vote in row.votes %}
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>{{ vote.voter }}</code></td>
                        <td><code>{{ vote.decision }}</code></td>
                        <td class="muted">{{ vote.reason ?: '—' }}</td>
                    </tr>
                {% else %}
                    <tr><td colspan="3" class="muted">No voter supported this request.</td></tr>
                {% endfor %}
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Verdict</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                {% for name, verdict in row.verdicts %}
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>{{ name }}</code></td>
                        <td>{{ verdict.granted ? '✅' : '⛔' }} <code>{{ verdict.decision }}</code></td>
                        <td class="muted">{{ verdict.reason }}</td>
                    </tr>
                {% endfor %}
            </tbody>
        </table>
    {% endfor %}

    <p class="muted">
        A locked document owned by someone on the editorial board is the interesting one: the owner
        grants, the board grants, compliance refuses. <code>permit_overrides</code> publishes it,
        <code>deny_overrides</code> does not, and <code>two_keys</code> does not either — a refusal
        is not outvoted there, which is the whole reason a second key was asked for.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
